\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The retained Gaussian marginal}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Use the nonempty split and positive blocks of
[Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D), with [measurable projection](fdip-002G)
#{\pi_R(x,y)=x} and \vocabk{effective precision}{fdip-000D}
#{S=A-HC^{-1}H^T}.
Let #{\mu_Q} and #{\mu_S} be the corresponding
\vocabk{Gaussian probability laws}{fdip-0005}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_Q&=c_C Z_S,\\
(\pi_R)_*\mu_Q&=\mu_S.
\end{aligned}}
The second identity says that the \newvocab{retained marginal law} is
#{\mu_S}: probabilities of events depending only on #{x} agree.
Every Borel measurable #{F:\mathbb R^R\to\mathbb R} satisfies
##{\begin{aligned}
F\in L^1(\mu_S)
 &\Longleftrightarrow F\circ\pi_R\in L^1(\mu_Q),\\
\langle F\rangle_S
 &=\langle F\circ\pi_R\rangle_Q.
\end{aligned}}
The expectation equality assumes either of the equivalent integrability
conditions.
Here #{L^1(\mu)} denotes Borel functions with finite integral of their
absolute value, and #{(\pi_R)_*\mu_Q} assigns a set #{B} the probability
#{\mu_Q(\pi_R^{-1}(B))}. This is the measure version of the
[finite pushforward law](ftip-007S).
The equality applies in particular to all polynomial retained
observables by \ref{fdip-0006}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.2}{rasmussen2006gaussian}, pp. 199-200,
gives formulas for Gaussian marginals and conditionals. Here the Gaussian
parameter is precision, so the inverse-block identification below
is needed to compare covariance formulas.}

\proof{
\p{The weights are continuous and nonnegative. Tonelli and the fiber
formula in \ref{fdip-000D} therefore give
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_Q
 &=\int_{\mathbb R^R}\int_{\mathbb R^U}
       e^{-(x,y)^TQ(x,y)/2}\,dy\,dx\\
 &=c_C\int_{\mathbb R^R}e^{-x^TSx/2}\,dx
  =c_CZ_S.
\end{aligned}}
All three factors are positive and finite. This also agrees with
#{\det Q=\det C\det S} and the normalization formula. For a Borel
set #{B\subseteq\mathbb R^R}, apply the same nonnegative calculation
with the factor #{\mathbf1_B(x)}. Dividing by #{Z_Q=c_CZ_S} gives
#{\mu_Q(\pi_R^{-1}(B))=\mu_S(B)}, which is the asserted pushforward.}

\p{To justify signed expectations directly, first apply Tonelli with
#{|F(x)|}. Even if either side is infinite, it yields
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\mathbb R^R\times\mathbb R^U}
 |F(x)|e^{-(x,y)^TQ(x,y)/2}\,dx\,dy\\
&\qquad=c_C\int_{\mathbb R^R}|F(x)|e^{-x^TSx/2}\,dx.
\end{aligned}}
Division by the positive normalizers proves the integrability
equivalence. When these absolute integrals are finite, Fubini
applies to the signed integrand with #{F(x)} in place of #{|F(x)|}.
The same fiber integral and normalization then give the expectation
identity. Thus a formal cancellation of iterated signed integrals
has been replaced by an explicit absolute-integrability check.}
}

\p{In particular the retained coordinate covariance is
##{\operatorname{Cov}_{\mu_Q}(x)=S^{-1}=(Q^{-1})_{RR}.}
The first equality is [covariance as inverse precision](fdip-000B)
for #{\mu_S}, together with the expectation identity. To verify the
block formula independently, solve #{Q(u,v)=(b,0)}. Its lower
equation gives #{v=-C^{-1}H^Tu}; the upper equation is then
#{Su=b}, so #{u=S^{-1}b}. Since this holds for every #{b}, the
upper-left block of #{Q^{-1}} is #{S^{-1}}. This is
\citet{Equation (A.13), p. 202}{rasmussen2006gaussian}.}

\p{The marginal is not the law obtained by fixing #{y=y_0}. For the
latter, #{A>0} follows by restricting #{Q} to #{(x,0)}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
&x^TAx+2x^THy_0\\
&\quad=(x+A^{-1}Hy_0)^T\\
&\qquad\cdot A(x+A^{-1}Hy_0)\\
&\qquad-y_0^TH^TA^{-1}Hy_0.
\end{aligned}}
Normalizing this density in #{x} gives conditional mean
#{-A^{-1}Hy_0} and conditional covariance #{A^{-1}}. These densities
form a conditional probability kernel: divide the joint probability
density #{p_Q(x,y)} by its positive #{y}-marginal density
#{r(y)=\int p_Q(x,y)\,dx}. Their product with #{r(y)} is the joint
density, which verifies the defining conditional integral identity.
Integrating
#{y} instead gives mean zero and covariance #{S^{-1}}. In
particular, setting #{y=0} retains precision #{A}, whereas
eliminating #{y} retains precision #{S}.}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.measurePreserving_restrict₂_multivariateGaussian}
gives coordinate restriction of a Gaussian specified by its covariance.
TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
gives the more general rectangular affine image. To apply either to
this statement, the input covariance is #{Q^{-1}}, and its retained
block must be identified with #{S^{-1}} as above. Mathlib's
\leanref{MeasureTheory.integral_prod} supplies Fubini under
integrability; it does not dispense with the preceding absolute-value
argument. These declarations do not by themselves identify the
precision density or its partition factor.}
